% ============================================================================
% reference-line-degree — the exact latitude MAGNITUDE a source states for a
% tropic, decoded from a clause already sitting unused inside
% `reference-lines.adj`'s own already-quoted NOAA NESDIS source sentence
% (adj-facts-stdlib, GEOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `reference-lines.adj`
% (`reference_line(line, marks)`, tropic_of_cancer → northernmost_sun_overhead,
% tropic_of_capricorn → southernmost_sun_overhead): that table's own
% already-quoted NOAA NESDIS span ALSO states the precise NUMERIC latitude of
% each tropic -- a fact the line/marks schema had no room for:
%
%     "One in the Northern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Cancer at +23.5°
%      latitude and one in the Southern Hemisphere called the Tropic of
%      Capricorn at − 23.5° latitude."
%
% New `reference_line_degree(line, degrees)` table decodes that span as its
% own rows: tropic_of_cancer → 23.5, tropic_of_capricorn → -23.5. "Degrees"
% (a precise signed numeric latitude) is categorically distinct from the
% parent table's "marks" (a categorical description of what the line marks) --
% the tropic of Cancer's marks value is northernmost_sun_overhead, but its
% numeric magnitude is a separate fact the marks column has no room for.
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? reference_line_degree(tropic_of_cancer, $Degrees)   % 23.5
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `reference_line_degree(line, degrees)` carrying the citation, so the
% lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query -- the engine returns the
% degree value WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and
% abstains on anything that is not one of these two tropics (it never
% invents a degree value). The query also runs BACKWARD -- bind a degree
% value and recall the line it names:
%
%     ? reference_line_degree($Line, -23.5)   % tropic_of_capricorn
%
% A recall on `equator` or `prime_meridian` -- real, already-tabled reference
% lines in `reference-lines.adj` whose own quoted spans DO state a numeric
% degree (0 degrees) -- correctly abstains here, because that exact numeric
% fact is already fully captured by their own `marks` atoms
% (`zero_degrees_latitude`/`zero_degrees_longitude`), so decoding it again
% into this table would restate the parent rather than add a new fact; this
% table exists specifically for the two tropics, whose numeric magnitude
% (23.5) is nowhere present in their `marks` atoms
% (`northernmost_sun_overhead`/`southernmost_sun_overhead`). A recall on
% `arctic_circle` or `antarctic_circle` -- real, already-tabled lines whose
% own quoted span never states a numeric degree at all (only "around the
% North/South Pole") -- also correctly abstains.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sentence already quoted
% inside `reference-lines.adj`'s own header, from the SAME NOAA NESDIS "What
% Is a Solstice?" page that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a different clause of that already-verified
% quote. `trust authoritative` -- the same tier the parent table already
% carries (NOAA NESDIS is a primary U.S. government reference).
% (See ../README.md.)
% ============================================================================

table reference_line_degree {
    columns line, degrees

    row (tropic_of_cancer, 23.5)
    row (tropic_of_capricorn, -23.5)

    source "One in the Northern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Cancer at +23.5° latitude and one in the Southern Hemisphere called the Tropic of Capricorn at − 23.5° latitude."
    locator "https://www.nesdis.noaa.gov/about/k-12-education/optical-phenomena/what-solstice"
    trust authoritative
}
